20 Apr 2026 09:45:15 am Mon
    - We did a fundamental rearchitecture of the system by adding a StringParser class
      to take the job of taking apart the user's numbers into the needed portions.  This
      was absolutely the right move and Mike was able to write parser revisions that
      deal with the complicated needs of the variety of ways we use to write numbers.
    - This was also aided by laying down strict expectations about what the incoming
      strings are allowed to look like.
        - Number and units separated by at least one space character
        - This allows rsplit(" ", 1) tool to get the unit.  No spaces allowed in unit.
        - Complex numbers:  <re>j, <im>j, or <re><sign><im>j.  No spaces are allowed
          within the complex expression.
        - Uncertainty:  only allowed the the standard short form notation
            - No '/' (division) allowed
            - The parenthetical portion (unc) must contain only digits (may be an
              arbitrary integer)
            - 'inf' and 'nan' are strictly prohibited within any uncertainty string
        - All space characters within a numeric string are stripped prior to parsing
        - Underscores (_) in numeric strings are allowed and ignored (Python float/int
          convention).
        - Rational Canonicalization: Rationals are stored as fractions.Fraction to
          ensure automatic reduction (e.g., 2/2 -> 1/1).
    - Where questions about behavior popped up, we used python's native behavior and the
      python uncertainties library to define the desired behavior.
    - The last core architectural changes were
        - Add NumType.UncCpx, a complex number with uncertainty & correlation
        - Add "1.00(1)-1.00(20)i<R=0.27>" notation for an UncCpx with correlation

